## Description of what I changed

**Before:** `ObsValidator` did two unrelated jobs at once. As well as reporting values that fall outside a concept&#39;s absolute range, it was the only thing in core that worked out an observation&#39;s reference range and interpretation and wrote them onto the `Obs`. Validators are only ever reached through `ValidateUtil.validate`, which returns immediately when the `validation.disable` global property is switched on. An implementation running with that property therefore saved every observation with no reference range and no interpretation at all — no error, no warning, just missing data that cannot be reconstructed afterwards, because the range criteria are evaluated against the patient&#39;s state at the time of the observation. The validator also overwrote any reference range a caller had already set on the observation.

**After:** deriving the reference range and the interpretation is a save-time concern, handled by a new `ObsSaveHandler`. Save handlers are dispatched by `RequiredDataAdvice` before, and independently of, validation, so an observation now gets its reference range whether validation is on or off. A reference range supplied by the caller is left alone rather than being replaced with a freshly derived one. `ObsValidator` keeps `validateAbsoluteRanges` and every `error.value.outOfRange.*` rejection; its remaining job is validation, and nothing else.

**How:** `setObsReferenceRange(Obs, ConceptReferenceRange)`, `setObsReferenceRange(Obs)` and `setObsInterpretation(Obs)` moved out of `ObsValidator` into `ObsSaveHandler`, which is registered as `@Handler(supports = Obs.class, order = 50)` and implements `SaveHandler`. The handler reproduces the guards that used to gate the population inside the validator: it skips voided observations, obs groups, non-numeric datatypes, numeric observations with no value, and observations with a null concept. Group members are covered because `RequiredDataAdvice.recursivelyHandle` already walks the `Obs.groupMembers` collection — the field carries neither `@Independent` nor `@DisableHandlers` — so the handler does not recurse itself. `ObsValidator` still looks the `ConceptReferenceRange` up for itself; reusing the one the handler derived is not safe, because `Obs.getReferenceRange()` is an `ObsReferenceRange` rather than a `ConceptReferenceRange`, becaus"}, {"evidence_id": "rebuild-20261014:90309a7359a4912d84fa7df6c8463b24e0ce5051:openmrs/openmrs-core:pullrequest:6560", "kind": "PullRequest", "path": null, "commit": null, "number": 6560, "title": "maven(deps): bump com.github.spotbugs:spotbugs-maven-plugin from 4.10.4.0 to 4.10.4.1", "email": null, "login": null, "name": null, "character_offset": 377, "excerpt": "2>
